{"author":{"id":"2f242a7e7f2592f8909954a77fabece74f6e8cc197182647ed3b15ea8e3878af"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791136895,"nonce":"DEuGSeEOX7vSzdF0D4rXKkNHMNQ=","title":"a short wait burns CPU on macOS","message":"Reported by ashui-8b: window_wait with a timeout of 8.3 ms costs about half a core while idle on macOS; at 100 ms it costs 1-2%.\n\nnext() does not spin: it pumps about once per wait. Each timed pump_app_events costs a fixed amount, so the cost scales with how often the program wakes. A sample of the idle bench showed where it goes:\n- winit wakes a timed pump with a CFRunLoopTimer whose interval is 0.1 us. Once it fires it re-fires on every pass of the run loop until the next pump re-arms it, and each re-fire re-arms a mach timer in the kernel (mk_timer_arm, __CFRunLoopDoTimers).\n- stop_app_immediately posts a dummy NSEvent, and AppKit's dequeue does LaunchServices paste telemetry (sysctl).\n\nIdle bench (600x400 window, device events off, M-series MacBook, 120 Hz display), CPU of one core:\n  wait 0.1     1%\n  wait 0.016   4-6%\n  wait 0.0083  15-17%   (ashui, with content: ~50%)\n  wait 0.004   26-31%\nsleep + poll instead of wait: 0.0083 12%, 0.004 41%.\n\nRefuted: peeking with nextEventMatchingMask(dequeue: NO) before pumping. Running the run loop outside a pump leaves winit's polling timer firing, which takes 100% of a core; and AppKit notifications delivered during the peek reach winit with no handler set and are dropped.\n\nFix: for a timed pump on macOS, add a one-shot CFRunLoopTimer at the deadline and pump with no timeout. winit's own timer stays parked, and the untimed pump still returns on the first wake: an event, a redraw request, or the alarm.\nAfter: wait 0.0083 2-3%, wait 0.004 3-4%. Waits per second unchanged, and mean lateness past the timeout 1.6 ms (was 2.8 ms) at 8.3 ms.\n\nEvery wait returns no sooner than about one display frame (120/s at 4 ms), before and after the fix.","files":null}]}